[s0l0!-header][h1]Function Parameters[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

For a function to use [b]arguments[/b], it must declare formal [b]parameters[/b], which are variables that accept the argument's values.

[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="cpp" ]void printSomething([b]int x[/b]) 
{
   cout << x;
}[/code]
This defines a function that takes one [b]integer [/b]parameter and prints its value.
[note]Formal parameters behave within the function similarly to other local variables. They are created upon entering the function, and are destroyed upon exiting the function.[/note]

